  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-MMR130</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Young women and men have improved  technical and vocational as well as core  competencies and access to labour market information</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2022, ILO estimated that 1.6 million jobs were lost after one year military takeover. Employment and jobs have become scarce and high percentage of skilled workers left the country. In 2024, survey by ILO and the World Bank showed that shortage of domestic labour and human capital in the country was caused by higher percentage of people choosing to migrate to neighbouring countries. The severe natural disaster and ongoing conflict have cast negative impacts on Myanmar&#x2019;s already weak economy. Families are carrying the burden of rising prices and workers are placed in very serious working condition. ILO support aims to provide social partners and TVET institution and CBOs with filling up skills gap in different sectors as well as equipping young people with high demand skills set. For this biennium, ILO is working with different TVET institutions and skills training centres to provide skills training to conflict and disaster effected communities and ensuring with partners that at least 50% of trained individuals engage in income-generating activities or enable to find employment using the skills they obtained from the training.</narrative>
  </description>
